#{[17592186074903 1029 "We can also use the standard algorithm to divide a four-digit number by a one-digit number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use the algorithm to divide $4,236$ by $3$. We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFirst, we consider the thousands:\n\n| $\\color{blue}1$$3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}4} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$\\color{lightgray}2$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}4} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}1}\\,\\text{R}\\,1$Write over thousands: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 3$Subtract: ${\\color{red}4} - 3 = 1$Bring down: $2$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds:\n\n| $1$$\\color{blue}4$$3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}3$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}12} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}4}$Write over hundreds: $\\color{blue}4$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 12$Subtract: ${\\color{red}12} - 12 = 0$Bring down: $3$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the tens:\n\n| $1$$4$$\\color{blue}1$$3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}6$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}3} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}1}$Write over tens: $\\color{blue}1$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}1} = 3$Subtract: ${\\color{red}3} - 3 = 0$Bring down: $6$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, we consider the ones:\n\n| $1$$4$$1$$\\color{blue}2$$3$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$1$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$3$$0$$\\color{red}6$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$\\fbox{0}$ | Divide: ${\\color{red}6} \\div 3 = {\\color{blue}2}$Write over ones: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $3 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6$Subtract: ${\\color{red}6} - 6 = \\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $4,236$, so the division is done. The number we have written at the top is the answer. There is no remainder.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4,236 \\div 3 = 1,412\n$$"] [17592186074904 1029 "We can use box models to divide numbers by two-digit numbers.\n\nLet's see how to use a box model by finding the value of $300 \\div 25$.\n\nWe start by writing our division in the following way. We place the dividend in a box and the divisor to the left of the box.\n\n|  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$ |  |  |\n\nWe want to find how many times $25$ goes into $300$. But instead of trying to figure this out in a single step, let's break this problem up into a series of simpler steps.\n\nIn our first step, we will pick a multiple of $25$ that is easy to compute and is fairly large but no larger than $300$. We will choose the multiple $25 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ because we can compute\n\n$$\n25 \\times {\\color{blue}10} = 250\n$$\n\nquickly and the result is no larger than $300$.\n\nTo organize our work so far, we write $\\color{blue}10$ above the box and subtract $25 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ from $300$ inside the box.\n\n|  | $\\color{blue}10$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$$-\\!$$2$$5$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ |  |  |\n\nThe result of the subtraction is ${\\color{red}50}$. This indicates that if we take $25$ a total of ${\\color{blue}10}$ times, then it will fit into $300$ with $\\color{red}50$ left over.\n\nNow, we repeat the process with a dividend of $\\color{red}50$ instead of $300$. To start the next round, we write $\\color{red}50$ in a new box to the right.\n\n|  | $10$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$5$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ | $\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ |  |\n\nAgain, we are looking for a multiple of $25$ that is easy to compute and is no larger than ${\\color{red}50}$. We will choose the multiple $25 \\times {\\color{blue}2}$ because we can compute\n\n$$\n25 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 50\n$$\n\nquickly and the result is no larger than ${\\color{red}50}$.\n\nAs before, we write $\\color{blue}2$ above the box and subtract $25 \\times {\\color{blue}2}$ from ${\\color{red}50}$ inside the box.\n\n|  | $10$ | $\\color{blue}2$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $25$ | $3$$0$$0$$-\\!$$2$$5$$0$$\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$ | $\\color{red}5$$\\color{red}0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |  |\n\nWe get $\\fbox{0}$, so the division is done.\n\nTo find the quotient $300 \\div 25$, all we need to do is add the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n10+2=12\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n300 \\div 25 = 12\\,\n$$"] [17592186074905 1029 "In this lesson, we'll consider real-life situations where we have to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nSuppose that Justin, a chef, has $8$ large tins of tomatoes. If each tin of tomatoes weighs\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb}\n$$\n\nhow many pounds of tomatoes does Justin have in total?\n\nTo find out the total weight of tomatoes, we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4}\\,\\textrm{lb}\n$$\n\nby $8$.\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n8 \\times \\dfrac54= \\dfrac{8\\times 5}{4} = \\dfrac{40}4\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by interpreting the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{4} = 40 \\div 4 = 10\n$$\n\nSo, in total, Justin has $10\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of tomatoes."] [17592186074906 1029 "Fraction multiplication occurs regularly in real-life situations.\n\nFor example, suppose that a bag contains\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\\,\\text{kg}\n$$\n\nof flour. If Nancy used\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nof the bag to make a pie, how much flour did she use to make the pie?\n\nTo find out how much flour Nancy used, we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nby\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTo multiply these two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} \\times \\dfrac{2}{5} = \\dfrac{3\\times 2}{5\\times 5} = \\dfrac{6}{25}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Nancy used\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{25}\n$$\n\nkilograms of flour."] [17592186074907 1029 "Fraction multiplication can often be interpreted as the area of a part of a unit square.\n\nLet's consider the unit square below. It has sides of length $1$ unit, divided into lots of little tiles.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/569/Source/Images/7b1a8f2f48c909e8319f2319fd5f6344.png)\n\nEach side of the unit square is divided into $\\color{blue}4$ equal pieces. So, each side of a tile is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nunits long.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/569/Source/Images/36e3503a5a0e8c93a90ecf313a2c2a43.png)\n\nThe area of each tile is the product of the two side lengths. Therefore, the area of each tile is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{1}{4}\\;\\textrm{square units}\n$$"] [17592186074908 1029 "Another way of computing the area of a part of a unit square is to realize that we can find the area of a single tile using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{area of a single tile} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{number of tiles}}\n$$\n\nKnowing this, what is the area of the shaded region below, in square units?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/569/Source/Images/8df1dffcd02ba30d75540506ef65101c.png)\n\nFirst, note that the region is $7$ tiles long and $7$ tiles high.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/569/Source/Images/e95db5ab9215bc444f8f355e3cfac7f1.png)\n\nSo there are a total of\n\n$$\n7 \\times 7 = 49\n$$\n\nsmall square tiles. Therefore, the area of each tile is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{49}\n$$\n\nsquare units.\n\nSince $10$ tiles are shaded, the total shaded area is:\n\n$$\n10\\times\\dfrac{1}{49} = \\dfrac{10}{49} \\,\\textrm{square units}\n$$"] [17592186074909 1029 "Instead of counting the number of tiles in a shaded region, we can find the length of its sides and multiply them together.\nSo, what is the area of the shaded part below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/569/Source/Images/e7931d0a2ac282ff74a125538f71c09e.png)\n\nFirst, notice that each side of the unit square is divided into $\\color{blue}7$ equal pieces. So, each of the smaller tiles has sides that are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\nunits long.\n\nThe shaded region is $4$ tiles long and $6$ tiles high, which means that it has dimensions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{7}\n$$\n\nunits by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nunits.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/569/Source/Images/51ea0164cf29bc28d15b64af8f21d908.png)\n\nFinally, we find the area of the shaded rectangle by multiplying the lengths of the sides. So, the area is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{7} \\times \\dfrac{6}{7}\\,\\,\\textrm{square units}\n$$"] [17592186074910 1029 "There are many ways to divide decimals. One such way is to write the division as a fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by a power of $10$ so that both become whole numbers. Then, we carry out the division as usual.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use this method to find the value of $4.5\\div 0.5$.\n\n**Step 1.** First, we write the division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4.5}{0.5}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Then, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4.5\\times 10}{0.5\\times 10} = \\dfrac{45}{5}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Finally, we solve the resulting division problem. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n45 \\div 5 = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4.5 \\div 0.5 = 9 \\,\n$$"] [17592186074911 1029 "When using long division to divide whole numbers, it's often appropriate to round the final answer.\n\nFor instance, let's find the value of $58 \\div 7$, rounded to the nearest tenth. We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{black}8} \\: \\ldots$$7$$\\phantom{\\mid}\\!\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$6$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$4$$\\vdots$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe could divide further, but since we want to round to $1$ decimal place (the nearest tenth), we stop after finding the quotient to $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n58 \\div 7 = 8.28 \\ldots\n$$\n\nFinally, we round the quotient to the nearest tenth. The hundredths place is $8$, which is greater than $5$, so we round up:\n$8.28 \\ldots \\rightarrow 8.3$"] [17592186074912 1029 "We can use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by whole numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $7.2 \\div 3$.\n\nFirst, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\,\\phantom{0} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.} 2{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}}}}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, we carry out the long division process as usual:\n\n| $\\,2 {\\,\\,. \\,} 4$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\,\\,. \\,} 2}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7.2 \\div 3 = 2.4 \\,\n$$\n\n**Note:** The \"standard algorithm\" is the same as \"long division.\" The two phrases can be used interchangeably."] [17592186074913 1029 "We can also use the standard algorithm to divide decimals by other decimals. The only difference is that we first need to find an equivalent division problem where the divisor is a whole number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's compute $1.11\\div 0.3 \\,$. First, we write this division problem as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11}{0.3}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10$ so that we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1.11 \\times 10}{0.3\\times 10} = \\dfrac{11.1}{3}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the division problem is equivalent to $11.1 \\div 3$. We can compute this using long division as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\, 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,\\,. \\,} 7$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\,\\,. \\,} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$2$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$1$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nFrom the long division, we find that\n\n$$\n11.1 \\div 3 = 3.7\n$$\n\nso we conclude that\n\n$$\n1.11 \\div 0.3 = 3.7 \\,\n$$"] [17592186074914 1029 "We can write ratios as fractions by placing the first number over the second number.\n\nSo, what ratio describes the number of rectangles to triangles below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/552/Source/Images/52d7ed8acdb780645278f3f87580d816.png)\n\nFirst, we count the number of rectangles. We see that there are $\\color{blue}4$ rectangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $\\color{red}3$ triangles.\n\nSo the ratio of rectangles to triangles is ${\\color{blue}4}:{\\color{red}3}$. As a fraction, this is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nWe interpret this number to mean that there are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nrectangles for each triangle."] [17592186074915 1029 "Sometimes, we are given a ratio and want to find an amount. In these cases, it's helpful to use equivalent fractions.\n\nFor example, if the ratio of cows to sheep on Margaret's farm is $11:7$, and she has $22$ cows, how many sheep are there on the farm?\n\nTo solve this, we start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}11}:{\\color{red}7}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}11}{\\color{red}7}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of cows and the denominator represents the number of sheep.\n\nSince there are $22$ cows on the farm, we would like a numerator of $22$. We make this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}11}\\times 2}{{\\color{red}7}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}22}}{{\\color{red}14}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is now the number of sheep on the farm if there are $22$ cows. Therefore, on Margaret's farm, there are $\\color{red}14$ sheep."] [17592186074916 1029 "Remember that we can place equivalent ratios in a ratio table, like the one below.\n\n| $\\color{blue}x$ | $\\color{red}y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ |\n| $\\color{blue}4$ | $\\color{red}8$ |\n\nBy setting the values in the left column as $\\color{blue}x$-coordinates and the values in the right column as the corresponding $\\color{red}y$-coordinates, we can graph the points to represent a relationship between the ratios.\n\nIn this case, we have the following ordered pairs: $({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}2})$, $({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}4})$, $({\\color{blue}4},{\\color{red}8})$.\n\nPlotting these points on a coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/561/Source/Images/d8dee400d56d83eff2607955df4071cf.png)\n\nConnecting the points gives us a straight line! Plotting equivalent ratios will *always* give a straight line. Furthermore, the straight line will *always* pass through the origin $(0,0)$."] [17592186074917 1029 "We can illustrate ratios with a **tape diagram**, where each box represents a unit.\n\nThe tape diagram below shows the ratio between the number of black hair ties and white hair ties there are in the package Anabelle bought.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/556/Source/Images/407e168e9dd0c86712c7b43bc43ada40.png)\n\nIn this diagram, each box represents a hair tie.\nSo, what is the ratio of white hair ties to black hair ties?\n\nThe tape diagram for white hair ties shows $\\color{blue}5$ boxes, so Anabelle has $\\color{blue}5$ white hair ties.\n\nLikewise, the tape diagram for black hair ties contains $\\color{red}6$ boxes, so she has $\\color{red}6$ black hair ties.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of white hair ties to black hair ties is ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}6}$."] [17592186074918 1029 "A **unit rate** is a comparison of two quantities where one of the terms has a quantity of $1$. This means the ratio, in fraction form, has a denominator of $1$.\n\nFor instance, suppose Michael earned $\\[math] 200$ by selling $4$ identical tires.\n\nThe ratio of dollars to tires is ${\\color{blue}200}:{\\color{red}4}$. Writing the ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200}}{{\\color{red}4}} \\,\n$$\n\nThe unit rate is how many dollars Michael got for $1$ tire.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200} \\div 4}{{\\color{red}4} \\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}50}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nThis is the unit rate. In this case, we say that Michael got $\\color{blue}50$ dollars *per* tire."] [17592186074919 1029 "In some tape diagrams, each box can represent more than one unit.\n\nFor instance, the ratio of pins to paper clips in a case is given by the tape diagram below. If there are $15$ pins in the case, how many paper clips are there in total?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/556/Source/Images/a84e49fe5cedc1ee9dd61edff4c395e9.png)\n\nWe are told that there are $15$ pins in the case, but the tape diagram for pins shows $3$ boxes. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents\n\n$$\n15 \\div 3 = 5\n$$\n\nunits.\n\nNow, we fill the number $5$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/556/Source/Images/a1b125a3119761dd8846e91d333930f2.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ boxes corresponding to paper clips, and each box represents $5$ units, the total number of paper clips is\n\n$$\n4 \\times 5 = 20 \\,\n$$\n\nSo, there are $20$ paper clips in Mrs. Robinson's case."] [17592186074920 1029 "A **percentage** is another way of expressing a fraction that's used often in everyday situations. The word **percent** means \"out of $100$.\"\n\nLet's take a look at the square below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/562/Source/Images/2d8d3e7cb3f6779fc6165ee7046fa39a.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the square has been divided into $100$ equal parts, and\n- $\\color{blue}50$ of those parts are shaded.\n\nSince $\\color{blue}50$ out of $100$ parts are shaded, we say that \"$\\color{blue}50$ percent of the square is shaded.\"\n\nWe have a special symbol that we use instead of the word percent. This symbol is written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Large{\\%}\n$$\n\nUsing this symbol, we say that \"${\\color{blue}{5}0}\\%$ of the square is shaded.\""] [17592186074921 1029 "When more than $100$ pieces are shaded in a model where $100$ pieces represent a whole, the percentage will be greater than $100\\%$.\n\nLet's take a look at the model below, where one large square represents one whole:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/562/Source/Images/7563b0ff0c869102a42fd6e9a1ea7276.png)\n\nIn this model, $170$ tiles are shaded. Therefore, this model represents $170\\%$."] [17592186074922 1029 "How do we find the percentage represented by a fraction model when the model isn't split into $100$ equal pieces?\n\nThe answer is to express the fraction as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $100$.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the model below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/562/Source/Images/a9a3c7d21cf1e0fb184f5e3abf8604c8.png)\n\nThis model represents the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTo make an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $100$, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $20$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 20}{5 \\times 20} = \\dfrac{80}{100}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{80}{100}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $80\\%$, we conclude that our fraction model represents $80\\%$.\n\nWe can also use equivalent fractions to construct a simplified fraction model for a given percent. Let's see an example."] [17592186074923 1029 "We can represent two-variable equations using graphs. For example, consider the following equation:\n\n$$\ny = x + 5\n$$\n\nA point $(x, y)$ is a solution to this equation if substituting the $x$-value into the equation gives the correct $y$-value.\n\nTo find some solution points, we can choose several $x$-values and compute the corresponding $y$-values.\nNotice that:\n\n- If $x=0$, then $y=0 + 5 = 5$.\n- If $x=1$, then $y=1 + 5 = 6$.\n- If $x=2$, then $y=2 + 5 = 7$.\n- If $x=3$, then $y=3 + 5 = 8$.\n\nWe can organize these results in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $5$ |\n| $1$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |\n\nSo, we have the following points:\n$(0,5),\\: (1,6),\\: (2,7),\\: (3,8)$\n\nTo graph the equation, we plot these points on the coordinate plane and draw a straight line through them.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/560/Source/Images/5857db47f833dd7ea2420de6b9ef91b5.png)\n\nEvery point $(x,y)$ on the line satisfies the equation."] [17592186074924 1029 "Two intersecting lines are **perpendicular** if they form a right angle (an angle that measures $90^\\circ$).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/521/Source/Images/3fc2d50e4339becae855531165b9cbe1.png)\n\nFor example, the two lines above are perpendicular because the angle $\\angle BOD$ is a right angle.\n\nThe opposite is also true: if two lines are perpendicular, then they meet at a right angle.\n\nAs you may have noticed already, when two lines are perpendicular, they don't form only one right angle. All the four angles formed by the intersection are right angles!\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/521/Source/Images/2c136dc7e1316b13531b6558fc71ffdd.png)"] [17592186074925 1029 "The distance between two points in the coordinate plane is the number of units between them. Let's explore two methods for calculating the horizontal distance between two points with the points $(1,1)$ and $(5,1)$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFirst, we plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/573/Source/Images/08f08da4bf92a93545e51e0c0838e0cf.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/573/Source/Images/64e452c4227adfa8cc18f504d550d973.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/573/Source/Images/25bb5c835012f844e078d32d76e8fac1.png)\n\nThe point $({\\color{blue}{1}},1)$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{1}$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $({\\color{red}{5}},1)$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{5}$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{5}} - {\\color{blue}{1}} = 4\n$$"] [17592186074926 1029 "When we want to find the distance between two points in different quadrants, method 1 works exactly the same, but method 2 is slightly different.\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis for a horizontal distance, or $x$-axis for a vertical distance, and then *adding*.\n\nSo, what's the distance between $(-3,2)$ and $(1,2)$?\n\n**Method 1**\n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/573/Source/Images/6ead0542500fe43d991f0603b149ec16.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/573/Source/Images/03d4884b05f30977d29a31e43621d9de.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/573/Source/Images/a51519bbf204fed70fac371543dfdfc3.png)\n\nThe point $({\\color{blue}{1}},2)$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{1}$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $({\\color{red}{-3}},2)$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{3}$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} = 4\n$$"] [17592186074927 1029 "We can combine transformations by applying them in turn.\n\nFor example, suppose that the segment $\\overline{AB}$ below is dilated with center $O$ and a scale factor $2$, and then shifted $3$ units down. What is the resulting segment?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/483/Source/Images/f51816fae3a5dce9b86c0b51a8989988.png)\n\nLet's apply the transformations in turn.\n\n- Dilating with center $O$ and scale factor $2$ gives the segment $\\overline{A'B'}$, shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/483/Source/Images/b48bdc1c72251a54075d3c9677a1c77e.png)\n\n- Next, we take $\\overline{A'B'}$ and shift it by $3$ units down. This gives the segment $\\overline{A''B''}$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/483/Source/Images/cb47e4c7d41e04f35864927587fd0de1.png)"] [17592186074928 1029 "In general, to prove that two polygons are congruent, we need to check that all pairs of corresponding sides are congruent and that all pairs of corresponding angles are congruent.\n\nWhen it comes to triangles, however, we have a few shortcuts.\n\nOne such shortcut is the **side-side-side (SSS) congruence criterion**, which states the following:\n\n> *Two triangles are congruent if and only if three sides of one triangle are congruent to three sides of the other triangle.*\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the triangles below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/531/Source/Images/a58e55553aa1a8460da73af61ef3a9d6.png)\n\nWe can see that we have three pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\overline{AB}\\cong \\overline{KL}, \\qquad \\overline{AC}\\cong \\overline{KM},\\qquad \\overline{BC}\\cong \\overline{LM}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the SSS congruence criterion guarantees that these triangles are congruent, and we can write\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle KLM\n$$\n\nThe SSS criterion saves us a lot of work. We didn't even have to check the angles!"] [17592186074929 1029 "The **angle-side-angle (ASA) congruence criterion** can be used as a shortcut to prove that two triangles are congruent. It states the following:\n\n> Two triangles are congruent if and only if two angles *and the included side* of one triangle are congruent to two angles and the included side of the other triangle.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the triangles below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/532/Source/Images/bc5bddd9139cecb03b195f0382044346.png)\n\nWe can see that two pairs of angles are congruent:\n\n$$\n\\angle{A}\\cong \\angle{K}, \\quad \\angle{B}\\cong \\angle{L} \\quad\n$$\n\nAlso, we see that the included sides are congruent:\n\n$$\n\\overline{AB}\\cong \\overline{KL}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ASA congruence criterion guarantees that these two triangles are congruent, and we can write\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle KLM\n$$"] [17592186074930 1029 "Two polygons are **similar** if they have the same shape (though they may have different sizes).\n\nMore precisely, two polygons are similar if the following conditions hold:\n\n1. Their corresponding angles are congruent.\n2. The ratios of the lengths of their corresponding sides are all equal.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle EFG$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/490/Source/Images/3d52e7a8ed9c3af8e7698eac4dc56862.png)\n\nFrom the figure above, we see that the corresponding angles are congruent:\n\n$$\n\\angle A \\cong \\angle E, \\qquad \\angle B \\cong \\angle F, \\qquad \\angle C \\cong \\angle G\n$$\n\nWe also need to check whether the ratios of the corresponding sides are the same. That is, we have to check whether\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{AB}{EF} &= \\frac{BC}{FG} = \\frac{AC}{EG}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the lengths of the sides, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{AB}{EF} &= \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{BC}{FG} &= \\frac{4}{8} = \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{AC}{EG} &= \\frac{5}{10} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ratios are the same. Therefore, we conclude that the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle EFG$ are similar. The common ratio of the sides,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis called the **similarity ratio**.\n\nTo express mathematically that the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle EFG$ are similar, we write\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\sim \\triangle EFG\n$$"] [17592186074931 1029 "Any transformation that maps a polygon to a similar polygon is called a **similarity transformation**.\n\nAny combination of transformations made up of translation, rotation, reflection, and dilation will result in a similarity transformation.\n\nTo understand why this is true, remember:\n\n- Rigid motions (rotation, reflection, and translation) do not change the lengths or angles of the original figure. They map polygons to congruent polygons. If two polygons are congruent, then they are similar.\n- A dilation with scale factor $k$ multiplies all the lengths of the original shape by $k$ (leaving the angle measures unchanged), thereby producing a similar shape."] [17592186074932 1029 "The **triangle proportionality theorem** states the following:\n\n> *If a line segment parallel to a side of a triangle intersects the other two sides, then the segment divides those sides proportionally.*\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose we have the triangle below, where $\\overline{DE}\\parallel \\overline{BC}$. How can we find the length of $\\overline{CE}$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/503/Source/Images/339388f5527c5307c3f119c2c2e50af2.png)\n\nNotice that the segment $\\overline{DE}$ is parallel to the side $\\overline{BC}$, and it intersects the other two sides, namely $\\overline{AC}$ and $\\overline{AB}$.\n\nTherefore, according to the triangle proportionality theorem, the segment $\\overline{DE}$ divides the two sides $\\overline{AC}$ and $\\overline{AB}$ proportionally:\n\n$$\n\\frac{AE}{CE} = \\frac{AD}{BD}\n$$\n\nWe can substitute the information from the diagram and then solve for $CE$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{AE}{CE} &= \\frac{AD}{BD} \\\\\n\\frac{2}{CE} &= \\frac{3}{6} \\\\\n12 &= 3CE \\\\\nCE &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186074933 1029 "The midpoint theorem is a special case of the triangle proportionality theorem.\n\nTo understand why, consider the situation when the line parallel to the side of the triangle bisects one of the other sides, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/503/Source/Images/abc60ed29b082ef193cd9524da0076de.png)\n\nBecause $\\overline{DE}\\parallel\\overline{AB}$, the triangle proportionality theorem tells us that the sides $\\overline{AC}$ and $\\overline{BC}$ of $\\triangle ABC$ are divided proportionally:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{CE}{EA} = \\dfrac{CD}{DB}\n$$\n\nWe also know that $EA=CE$, so\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{CE}{EA} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{CE}{EA} &= \\frac{CD}{DB} \\\\\n1 &= \\frac{CD}{DB} \\\\\nCD &= DB\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, if the parallel line bisects one of the sides, it must bisect the other side as well.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/503/Source/Images/500f267a9e5c11c63237613171f0489b.png)\n\nGoing in the other direction, we can also state that if a line segment connects the midpoints of two sides of a triangle, it must be parallel to the third side."] [17592186074934 1029 "The **perpendicular bisector theorem** states that:\n\n> In a plane, if a point is on the perpendicular bisector of a segment, then it is equidistant from the endpoints of the segment.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, we see that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/521/Source/Images/230f956f1b30b8cccda096f4d41df05f.png)\n\nConsequently, we can conclude that the point $D$ is equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}$. So we have $AD=BD$."] [17592186074935 1029 "The **converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem** states that:\n\n> In a plane, if a point is equidistant from the endpoints of a segment, then it is on the perpendicular bisector of the segment.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, we see that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is perpendicular to $\\overline{AB}$, and the point $D$ is equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/521/Source/Images/66b019c9609135b41ac4a66d0041c6d1.png)\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$. In particular, we conclude that $AC = BC$."] [17592186074936 1029 "Let's consider a circle with center $O$ and two distinct points $A$ and $B$ on the circumference.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/550/Source/Images/f9c15865b45fb47cac624c0a81a62067.png)\n\nRecall that the points $A$ and $B$ determine two distinct arcs on the circle:\n\n- The **minor arc** $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ consists of $A$, $B$, and all points *on* the circle that lie *inside* $\\angle AOB$.\n- The **major arc** $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ consists of $A$, $B$, and all points *on* the circle that lie *outside* $\\angle AOB$.\n\nLet's refer to the following diagram, where\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOB = 80^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/550/Source/Images/105e4cdce7483bf988ae4526a76f7885.png)\n\nHere, $\\angle AOB$ is called the **central angle** corresponding to the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$.\n\nIn general, a minor arc is said to have the same measure as its central angle. Here, the minor arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ has the central angle $\\angle AOB$. Therefore, the measure of the arc is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmAB^(⌢) &= m∠AOB = 80^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since there are $360^\\circ$ in a full circle, the major arc $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ must have a measure of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmACB^(⌢) &= 360^{∘} - m∠AOB \\\\\n&= 360^{∘} - 80^{∘} \\\\\n&= 280^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLabeling the measures of the arcs without explicitly labeling the central angle is common. For example:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/550/Source/Images/baafc6a338c57893702f0c67efa76b3d.png)\n\n**Note**: When we use arc notation with only two letters (e.g., $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$), this always refers to the *minor* arc."] [17592186074937 1029 "Recall that for a circle of radius $r$, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nand the circumference is given by\n$C = 2\\pi r$.\n\nWhen solving problems that involve circles, we often need to use the formula for the area of a circle *and* the formula for the circumference.\n\nFor instance, how do we find the area of a circle given that it has a circumference of\n\n$$\nC=8\\pi\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/494/Source/Images/ea585b80de6e040a49ac97b0425c1005.png)\n\nThe formula for the circumference of a circle is $C = 2\\pi r$, where $r$ is the radius. Using the fact that $C = 8\\pi$, we can solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= 2πr \\\\\n8π &= 2πr \\\\\n8π &= 2πr \\\\\n8 &= 2r \\\\\nr &= 4cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n&= π \\cdot 4^{2} \\\\\n&= 16πcm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186074938 1029 "Recall that any two radii of a circle form an arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ and a sector $OAB$ with central angle $\\angle AOB$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/494/Source/Images/2ba4f30c93b5210fb23b0e3ac393ca61.png)\n\nThe key idea for solving problems that involve circular sectors is to work with the ratio of the central angle $m\\angle{AOB}$ to that of a full turn of $360^\\circ$. Let\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{m\\angle{AOB}}{360^\\circ}\n$$\n\nand let $\\mathcal{A}$ and $C$ denote the area of the circle and its circumference, respectively. Then,\n\n- the length of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is $k \\cdot C$, and\n- the area of the sector is $k \\cdot \\mathcal{A}$."] [17592186074939 1029 "Consider the right triangle below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/510/Source/Images/70c76419d42125670efbdd1e14370cb7.png)\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sin A = \\frac{BC}{AC}, & \\cos C = \\frac{BC}{AC}, \\\\ \\cos A = \\frac{AB}{AC}, & \\sin C = \\frac{AB}{AC}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\sin A = \\cos C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\cos A = \\sin C\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\nm\\angle A =\\theta\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\angle A$ and $\\angle C$ are complementary, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle C = 90^\\circ - \\theta\n$$\n\nand we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= \\cos (90^{∘} - θ) \\\\\n\\cos θ &= \\sin (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n- the sine of an angle equals the cosine of its complement, and\n- the cosine of an angle equals the sine of its complement.\n\nSine and cosine are known as **cofunctions**. The latter is called **co**sine to indicate that it is the **co**function of sine."] [17592186074940 1029 "We've seen how to apply trigonometric ratios to angles given in degrees, but we can also apply them to angles given in radians.\n\nFor example, let's consider the expression\n$\\cos({\\color{red}1})$.\nNote that $\\color{red}1$ is written without any special symbols, like ${}^\\circ$ (degrees). This indicates that the angle $\\color{red}1$ is given in *radians*. Here, $\\color{red}1$ reads as \"one radian.\"\n\nIf we were given $\\cos({\\color{blue}1^\\circ})$ instead, with the degree sign ${}^\\circ$, that would mean we want the cosine of \"one degree.\"\n\nThe simplest way to find the actual value of $\\cos(1)$ would be using a calculator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\cos(1) = 0.540\\,302\\ldots \\approx 0.540\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n**Watch out!** Don't forget to set your calculator into the \"radians\" mode before computing the trigonometric function above."] [17592186074941 1029 "In the diagram below, $\\triangle ABC$ is **inscribed** in a circle with center $O$. This means that all three of the triangle's vertices lie on the circle's circumference.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/517/Source/Images/6211fa4c51ee009df2576ac4510d3488.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we can see that $\\overline{AB}$ passes through the point $O$. So, $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle.\n\nBased on this information, **Thales' theorem** tells us that the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ *must* be a right triangle. More precisely:\n\n> *If $A, B$, and $C$ are distinct points on a circle where $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter, then $m\\angle C = 90^\\circ$.*\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/517/Source/Images/cfe2fee8c6bb56587e64a9bb83246a08.png)\n\nNow that we know $\\triangle ABC$ is a right triangle, we can apply the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of the hypotenuse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AC^{2} + BC^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{8^{2} + 6^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{64 + 36} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{100} \\\\\n&= 10cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186074942 1029 "We can use Thales' theorem to calculate missing angles in inscribed triangles. For example, consider the figure below. We have a triangle inscribed in a circle, and $AC$ is a diameter.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/505/Source/Images/2973b64b22c0536512fd06614a603caf.png)\n\nBecause $\\triangle ABC$ is an inscribed triangle and its side $AC$ is a diameter of the circle, Thales' theorem tells us that $\\triangle ABC$ must be a right triangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/505/Source/Images/ab715f13ca2348a840d22f544c2b3e24.png)"] [17592186074943 1029 "Consider the quadrilateral $ABCD$ that's inscribed in the circle below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/506/Source/Images/4e0a97753221eb4c65284af670a214a5.png)\n\nFor any quadrilateral inscribed in a circle, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *A quadrilateral can be inscribed in a circle if and only if both pairs of opposite angles are supplementary*.\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠C &= m∠B + m∠D = 180^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n75^{∘} + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠C &= 180^{∘} - 75^{∘} \\\\\nm∠C &= 105^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186074944 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/514/Source/Images/31d013f8a0f8159478f82d66e83202d8.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, two tangents to the circle meet at the point $P$. The segments $\\overline{AP}$ and $\\overline{BP}$ are called **tangent segments** to the circle.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Since $\\overline{AP}$ and $\\overline{BP}$ are tangent segments, we have that $\\angle OAP$ and $\\angle OBP$ are right angles.\n- $\\overline{OA}$ and $\\overline{OB}$ are congruent because they are radii of the circle.\n- $OP$ is a common hypotenuse of $\\triangle OAP$ and $\\triangle OBP$.\n\nTherefore, $\\triangle{OAP}$ and $\\triangle{OBP}$ are also congruent by the HL (Hypotenuse-Leg) criterion.\n\n$$\n\\triangle OAP \\cong \\triangle OBP\n$$\n\nAs a consequence, we obtain\n$AP = BP$.\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important property:\n\n> Two tangent segments with a common endpoint in a circle's exterior are congruent."] [17592186074945 1029 "Given a circle, an **inscribed angle** is an angle whose vertex lies on the circumference and whose sides are chords that meet at that vertex.\n\nIn the diagram below, the points $P$, $A$, and $B$ define the inscribed angle $\\angle APB$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/497/Source/Images/9b5540897b223e7a01337b1ad69d8955.png)\n\nThe arc formed by the endpoints of an inscribed angle is called an **intercepted arc**. Here, the endpoints $A$ and $B$ determine the intercepted arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$.\n\nRemember that the measure of an arc is always equal to the measure of the corresponding central angle. Here, we have\n\n$$\nm\\overset{\\frown}{AB} = m\\angle{AOB}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/497/Source/Images/c28e079e17287035ddb5fec15f97240a.png)"] [17592186074946 1029 "The **inscribed angle theorem** states the following:\n\n> *The measure of an inscribed angle is half the measure of the central angle of the same intercepted arc.*\n\nTo illustrate, consider the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/497/Source/Images/9363c6e95e2e072ee12b76e39792a042.png)\n\nBy the inscribed angle theorem, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle APB = \\dfrac{1}{2} m \\angle AOB\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nm \\angle APB = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 100^\\circ = 50^\\circ\n$$\n\nThis gives the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/497/Source/Images/9553e0505409858394ee9f06bdcb5859.png)"] [17592186074947 1029 "So far, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= \\cos (90^{∘} - θ), \\\\\n\\cos θ &= \\sin (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the above properties, we can also derive cofunction properties for secant and cosecant. We start with\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sec θ &= \\frac{1}{\\cos θ} \\\\\n&= (1)/(\\sin (90^{∘} - θ)) \\\\\n&= \\csc (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the same reasoning, we can show that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc θ &= \\sec (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have deduced that secant and **co**secant are also cofunctions. To summarize:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sec θ &= \\csc (90^{∘} - θ), \\\\\n\\csc θ &= \\sec (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by similar reasoning, it's possible to show that tangent and **co**tangent are cofunctions. Their cofunction relationships are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan θ &= \\cot (90^{∘} - θ), \\\\\n\\cot θ &= \\tan (90^{∘} - θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186074948 1029 "Another way to compute a trigonometric ratio of an angle, say $\\sin\\theta$ where $\\theta$ is measured in radians, is to convert the angle into degrees first and then compute the trigonometric ratio of the equivalent angle in degrees.\n\nLet's consider the value of\n\n$$\n\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince the angle is a multiple of $\\pi$, we can easily convert the measure in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}\\right) \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \\dfrac {180^\\circ} {10} = 18^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, using a calculator in \"degrees\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (\\frac{π}{10}) &= \\sin (18^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 0.309016… \\\\\n&\\approx 0.309\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186074949 1029 "The most important property of perpendicular lines is the following.\n\n> Given a line $l$ and a point $P$, there is one and only one line $m$ that passes through $P$ and is perpendicular to $l$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/521/Source/Images/f5dbcae901ecbd915c2540ec7d4648fa.png)\n\nOther nice properties include the following:\n\n- If two distinct lines are perpendicular to the same (third) line, then these two lines are parallel. For example, in the figure below, both lines $n$ and $m$ are perpendicular to the line $l$. As a result, $n \\parallel m$.\n- If a line is perpendicular to one of two parallel lines, then that line is also perpendicular to the second parallel line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/521/Source/Images/93f4d84a21580859479b327609c17a17.png)\n\nSymbol $\\perp$\n\nFinally, if a line $p$ is perpendicular to a line $q$, we can denote it without words using the symbol $\\perp$ (perpendicular), as follows:\n\n$$\np \\perp q\n$$\n\nThis reads as \"*$p$ is perpendicular to $q$*.\""] [17592186074950 1029 "A segment **bisector** is any segment, line, or ray that splits another segment into two congruent parts.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ is a bisector of the segment $\\overline{ST}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/521/Source/Images/bc413be9b98c4cbcf7a7245874428d9e.png)\n\nA **perpendicular bisector** is any segment, line, or ray that bisects another segment *and is perpendicular to that segment*.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{UV}$ is a perpendicular bisector of the segment $\\overline{ST}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/521/Source/Images/fd32cb7e46aac47ac789fb596d565a76.png)"] [17592186074951 1029 "In this lesson, we will learn how to divide larger numbers by two-digit numbers *with remainders* using the relationship between multiplication and division.\n\nTo begin with, let's figure out how to compute $47\\div 13$.\n\nWe will start by computing products of $13$ and other whole numbers, starting from $1$, until we get to a number that is greater than $47$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n13 \\cdot 1 &= 13 \\\\\n13 \\cdot 2 &= 13 + 13 = 26 \\\\\n13 \\cdot 3 &= 26 + 13 = 39✓ \\\\\n13 \\cdot 4 &= 39 + 13 = 52\\cdot([math]> {47}[/math], so too big)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $13 \\times {\\color{blue}3}$ is *smaller than* $47$, but $13 \\times 4$ is *greater than* $47$. This means that ${47}\\div {13}$ has a whole number part (or quotient) of ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nTo find the remainder, we subtract the product from the dividend:\n\n$$\n{47} - {39} = {\\color{red}8}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{47}\\div {13} = {\\color{blue}3}\\,\\textrm{R}\\,{\\color{red}8}\n$$"] [17592186074952 1029 "Unit rates can be used to solve many real-world problems in which we are given two quantities and wish to find what happens to one quantity when we vary the other.\n\nFor example, we might know the total cost of a specific number of items and be asked to find the cost for a different number of the same item.\n\nTo solve a problem using unit rates, the first step is to compute the unit rate by dividing the two given quantities. Then, we can use the unit rate to calculate the desired quantity.\n\nLet's see some examples!"] [17592186074953 1029 "A **polynomial equation** is an equation that can be written in the form\n$P_n(x)=0$,\nwhere $P_n(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $n$.\nA **cubic equation** is a polynomial equation of degree $n=3$.\n\nTo solve a polynomial equation with no constant term, we factor the polynomial part using the greatest common factor.\nOnce we've done that, we can apply the zero product rule to the resulting factors.\n\nFor instance, consider the cubic equation $x^3-x=0$.\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $x$. We can factor out the $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-1$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x^{2} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 1)(x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, we conclude that the solutions are\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x=0}\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x = -1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}x=1}\n$$"] [17592186074954 1029 "A **biquadratic equation** is a polynomial of degree $4$ that has only even powers of the variable. For example,\n\n- the equation $a^{\\color{blue}4}-3a^{\\color{blue}2}+2=0$ is a biquadratic equation because the exponents $\\color{blue}4$ and $\\color{blue}2$ are both even, while\n- the equation $a^{\\color{blue}4}+3a^{\\color{blue}1}+2=0$ is *not* a biquadratic equation because the exponents $\\color{blue}4$ and $\\color{blue}1$ are *not* both even.\n\nSolving a biquadratic may seem daunting because of the higher powers, but there is a trick! We just substitute a new variable in the place of the original variable squared.\n\nFor example, to solve the equation\n\n$$\na^4-3a^2+2=0\n$$\n\n, just let $b = a^2$ and substitute $b$ in the place of $a^2$. Then, we can solve for $b$ as follows:\n\nLet's work it out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a^4-3a^2+2 &= 0 \\ \\left(a^2\\right)^2 - 3\\left(a^2\\right) +2 &= 0 \\\\ (b)^2 - 3(b) + 2 &= 0 \\\\ b^2 - 3b + 2&= 0 \\\\ (b-1)(b - 2) &= 0 \\\\ b &= 1,2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have the solutions $b=1,2$. However, the original equation was given in terms of $a$, so we need to find the corresponding values of $a$. To do this, we substitute $a^2$ back in for $b$ and solve for $a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}a^{2} &= 1 & ⇒ & a|= ± 1 \\\\ a^{2}|= 2 & ⇒ & a|= ± \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\na = \\pm 1, \\pm \\sqrt{2}\n$$"] [17592186074955 1029 "We can partition any segment in a given ratio using techniques like those discussed for horizontal and vertical segments.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the point $C$ on the segment from the point $A(-1,0)$ to the point $B(7,4)$ that divides the segment into the ratio $1:1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/527/Source/Images/37b352ecbc82b34e46a6c4fc6f67228e.png)\n\nPartitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ into a ratio $a:b$ means we must\n\n- divide the segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $1:1$ implies\n\n$$\nAC:CB = 1:1\n$$\n\nThis means\n\n- there are $1+1 = 2$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{2}AB$.\n\nNext, consider another point $X$ that is horizontal with $A$ and directly above $C$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/527/Source/Images/ac56fa7e81e5e7428694908c142662fb.png)\n\nTo go from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" horizontally from $A$ to $X$ and then \"rise\" vertically from $X$ to $C$.\n\nSo, we compute the coordinates of $C$ as follows:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$x_{C}|= x_{A} + \\frac{1}{2}(x_{B} - x_{A}); =-1 + \\frac{1}{2}(7 - (-1)); =-1 + \\frac{8}{2}; = 3$.\n- The $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$y_{C}|= y_{A} + \\frac{1}{2}(y_{B} - y_{A}); = 0 + \\frac{1}{2}(4 - 0); = \\frac{4}{2}; = 2$.\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 2 \\right)\n$$"] [17592186074956 1029 "When writing a similarity statement, corresponding vertices should appear in the same position in the statement. For example, if we're given that\n\n$$\nABC \\sim PQR\n$$\n\nfor two polygons $ABC$ and $PQR$, we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n\\angle A \\cong \\angle P, \\qquad \\angle B \\cong \\angle Q, \\qquad \\angle C \\cong \\angle R\n$$\n\nWe can also deduce that\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{AB}{PQ}= \\dfrac{BC}{QR} = \\dfrac{AC}{PR}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the similarity ratio."] [17592186074957 1029 "As a second example, let's consider the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/497/Source/Images/96b118bd147c8e9a80bffb5182518451.png)\n\nSince $\\angle P$ and $\\angle Q$ are inscribed angles with the same intercepted arc, we have that\n\n$$\nm \\angle P = m \\angle Q\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=y$, and we now have the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/497/Source/Images/c740f05349cf1a6f65fab9e7666e720e.png)\n\nMoreover, since\n\n$$\nm\\overset{\\frown}{AB}=80^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOB = 80^\\circ\n$$\n\nand by the inscribed angle theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠P = m∠Q &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot m∠AOB \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 80^{∘} \\\\\n&= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFilling in this information, we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/497/Source/Images/54eaea43844c9dcd67ff778241df74de.png)"] [17592186074958 1029 "The formula for the distance between two points in three-dimensional Cartesian space is similar to the one we know for the two-dimensional Cartesian plane, only now we have three coordinates instead of two.\n\nSo, the distance $d$ between two points $A(x_1,y_1,z_1)$ and $B(x_2,y_2,z_2)$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2 + (y_2-y_1)^2 + (z_2-z_1)^2}\n$$\n\nLet's consider, for example, the points $A(2, 1, 3)$ and $B(5, 0, -7)$. Then, $A$ and $B$ have coordinates\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}1},3)}_{\\!({\\color{blue}x_1},{\\color{red}y_1},z_1)}, \\qquad \\underbrace{({\\color{blue}5},{\\color{red}0},-7)}_{\\!({\\color{blue}x_2},{\\color{red}y_2},z_2)}\n$$\n\nrespectively. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{(5 - 2)^{2} + (0 - 1)^{2} + (-7 - 3)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + (-1)^{2} + (-10)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 1 + 100} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{110}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186074959 1029 "Recall that the product rule for radicals states that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a\\cdot b} = \\sqrt{a}\\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are nonnegative numbers.\n\nWe can use the product rule for radicals to simplify some expressions. Let's see an example."] [17592186074960 1029 "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]2 - 6\\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\sqrt[3]{2} - 6\\sqrt[3]{2} &=  \\\\\n(4 - 6)\\sqrt[3]{2} &=  \\\\\n&= - 2\\sqrt[3]{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[3] 2 + \\sqrt[3] 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3] 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 5$.\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms -- not even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 2$."] [17592186074961 1029 "Let's remind ourselves of the symbols we use to compare numbers:\n\n- the symbol \"$>$\" means \"is greater than\"\n- the symbol \"$<$\" means \"is less than\"\n- the symbol \"$=$\" means \"is equal to\"\n\nWe can also use these symbols to compare fractions.\n\nFor example, let's compare the following fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nThe area models for these fractions are shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/538/Source/Images/a4523d626d969337e8d388e1fa391b60.png)\n\nTo compare these fractions, we note the following:\n\n- The fractions have the *same number of parts* (thirds). This means we only need to compare the number of *shaded* parts.\n- Since the fraction on the left has *fewer* shaded parts, it must be *smaller* than the fraction on the right.\n\nTo express the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nis smaller than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac23\n$$\n\nwe use a \"less than\" symbol, as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/538/Source/Images/c9630cc757a87c8b4de7a003c608fab5.png)\n\nFinally, expressing this comparison using numbers only, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\,< \\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$"] [17592186074962 1029 "When two fraction models have the same number of shaded parts, we compare the size of the parts.\n\nFor example, let's compare the following fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\qquad \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing the fraction models.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/538/Source/Images/c65fe0e2a5274ff61437853b1979ef03.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- both fractions have one shaded part, and\n- the fraction on the left has *larger* parts (halves) than the fraction on the right (thirds).\n\nThis means that the fraction on the left is *larger* than the fraction on the right. Let's add a \"greater than\" symbol to show this.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/538/Source/Images/97f73401724ae0bc93d70ba697348d2f.png)\n\nWriting this inequality using numbers only, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} > \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$"] [17592186074963 1029 "We can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 13 \\times 2\n$$\n\nFirst, we represent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nusing a model:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/564/Source/Images/cff0265ca8e97395f8b6cf70a6b8f346.png)\n\nMultiplication by a whole number is simply a repeated addition. And, since we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{3}\n$$\n\nby ${\\color{blue}2}$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}2$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/564/Source/Images/762ccc6debd80c00afb886b8bda8505b.png)\n\nThere are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 2\n$$\n\nshaded pieces in total. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$"] [17592186074964 1029 "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the multiplication problem below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10} \\times 7\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/564/Source/Images/aa072d0e01b4c54c4965e36d9bec2d74.png)\n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $7$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $7$.\n\nOur model has $1$ shaded piece. So, the resulting model must have\n\n$$\n1 \\times 7 = 7\n$$\n\nshaded pieces:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/564/Source/Images/616c9ebe36cb792390535567284fa99d.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\\times 7 = \\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$"] [17592186074965 1029 "We can use area models to understand what it means to divide a fraction by a whole number.\n\nFor example, let's consider the area model below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/568/Source/Images/da1076743ff376532dfd94f5ec4f237e.png)\n\nThe area of a rectangle equals its length multiplied by its width.\n\nFor the given rectangle:\n\n- its length is $3$\n- its width is $\\dfrac{1}{12}$\n- its area is $\\dfrac{1}{4}$\n\nTherefore, this area model shows the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n3\\times \\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can turn this multiplication problem into the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12} = \\dfrac14\\div 3\n$$\n\nWe can also use area models to interpret the division of a whole number by a fraction. Let's see an example."] [17592186074966 1029 "Whenever two right triangles are similar, their trigonometric ratios are equal for corresponding angles. This provides a convenient tool for solving problems involving lengths and angles in similar right triangles.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the two triangles shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/513/Source/Images/5fde56e05b874f9d63d3543ee489ce5b.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to compute the following trigonometric ratio:\n\n$$\n\\tan D\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it may seem that we don't have enough information to find $\\tan D$ since both the opposite side, $EF$, and the adjacent side, $DF$, are unknown.\n\nHowever, we can compute $\\tan D$ by showing that the two triangles are similar and then finding the tangent of the angle corresponding to $D$.\n\nNotice we have two congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\angle B\\cong \\angle F, \\qquad \\angle C \\cong \\angle E\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two triangles are similar by the AA (Angle-Angle) criterion:\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\sim \\triangle DFE\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\angle A\\cong\\angle D\n$$\n\nNotice that we have enough information to find $\\tan A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan A = \\frac{BC}{AB} = \\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ and $D$ have equal measure, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\tan D = \\dfrac43\n$$"]}
